[38;5;231m⏺[39m That settles behavior. Last major question before I put a design together: [1mthe stack.[0m

  The repo is one static HTML file, so effectively everything is a choice. You need a server, a relational store,
  session/auth, migrations, and a background worker — and most of the work in sub-project 1 is the boring half of that
  list. A batteries-included framework hands you auth, sessions, an ORM, migrations, and an admin UI on day one; a
  minimal framework gives you a smaller, more legible codebase but you assemble and maintain those pieces yourself.

  I notice your global config is set up around Python (micromamba + uv, ruff, mypy, reStructuredText docstrings), so I'm
  weighting Python-first unless you tell me otherwise.

  Recommendation: [1mDjango + Postgres[0m, server-rendered templates with a small amount of JavaScript for the notification
  bell. It covers auth, permissions, migrations, and admin — which is most of sub-project 1 — for free, and it leaves
  the interesting design work where it belongs, on the subscription and notification models. For the worker, the
  lightest thing that fits (a simple queue, not a Celery deployment).

  Two notes: SQLite is genuinely fine at this scale and swapping to Postgres later is routine, so don't read the
  database as a heavy commitment. And whatever you pick, the frontend stays server-rendered — a single-page app is a
  second project's worth of work for an internal task list.
